explanation:
The body takes `g.toIntLinearMap.lTensor (S.nonDegenerate n →₀ ℤ)`, which is exactly id ⊗ g on (free ℤ-module on the nondegenerate n-simplices) ⊗_ℤ A. It wraps this in the `ULift` equivalences that the carrier `tensorNormalizedChainComplexX` needs.

The universe levels agree on both sides. The source lives at `max a w (max b p)` and the target at `max b w (max a p)`, and these are the same level. So the padding is only bookkeeping.

The lemmas in the file confirm that this is the intended map:
- on pure tensors it sends z ⊗ x to z ⊗ g x;
- it is functorial in g, additive, and preserves exactness;
- it commutes with the boundary;
- it equals the degree-n component of the normalized coefficient morphism, after conjugating by the comparison isomorphism.

Generality: chains with coefficients in an arbitrary abelian group, given as an integral tensor product, are the standard setting for H_n(X;A). One could also allow R-modules over a commutative ring R, but this library's construction is deliberately integral, with abelian-group coefficients, so this is not too narrow. Mathlib has the general `LinearMap.lTensor`, which the definition uses, but no change-of-coefficients map for tensor normalized chains of simplicial sets.
